plague, plague the country today.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:35.370
How far back does this sort of oligarchical
apparatus exist in the history of the United

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.390
States?

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:46.410
Well, it goes back to arguably Plymouth Rock
and slightly before, but, yeah, because we

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:52.550
fought a war, you know, the Revolutionary
War against the oligarchy of the United Kingdom,

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:58.320
it was called a monarchy, but it's just another
version of oligarchy, oligarchy being defined

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.109
broadly as rule by the rich.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:05.970
The consolidation of political power into
a small number of very wealthy hands is sort

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:11.500
of the political version of what Monopoly
is in the economic marketplace, you know,

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:18.760
and and then we you know, we've had we're
now in the midst of our third real internal

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:25.260
challenge by oligarchs in the era from the
30s to the eighteen sixties, largely as a

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:31.770
result of the invention of the cotton gin,
the South consolidated massively.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:37.120
Small farmers, particularly cotton farmers,
were wiped out, large plantations and their

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.500
owners rose up and took over the entire political
structure of the South declared war on the

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.630
north, didn't like that pesky democracy next
door that was agitating and rabble rousing

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.970
among their ranks.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:56.560
And, you know, we we fought a bloody war against
these oligarchs and defeated them and in fact,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.030
broke up their plantations.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.480
Parchment is now a state prison in Louisiana.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:07.200
Robert E. Lee's plantation, one of the largest,
is now Arlington National Cemetery, etc..

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.590
So and then they rose up again in the eighteen,
eighties and nineties with the industrial

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.560
revolution, a new generation of oligarchs.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.629
And to the point that, you know, in the nineteen
thirties, in the early 90s, in nineteen thirty

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:26.599
three, they tried to hire Smedley Butler to
kidnap or assassinate Franklin Roosevelt.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:31.750
They already had enlisted over one hundred
thousand right wing veterans out of this right

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.959
wing veterans group to march on the White
House and seize him.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:37.269
We overthrew that.

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:42.269
And and Roosevelt openly said, you know, they
hate me and I welcome their hatred.

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:47.120
He put the oligarchs back in the bottle and
they stayed there until the nineteen eighties.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.110
Reagan let them back out again, along with
a little help from the Supreme Court.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:58.000
And today, we're now facing the third major
confrontation between oligarchy and democracy.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.170
And the study from Gillinson page suggests
that we are now in a full blown oligarchy.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:10.440
And the question facing America today is,
do we continue going down that road or do

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:16.689
we go back to democracy because oligarchies
are very unstable and oligarchs tend to oligarchies

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.950
tend to resolve themselves in one of two ways.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:25.079
Either the oligarchs are defeated, as we did
in the 60s and as we did in the nineteen thirties,

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:30.540
or the oligarchs turn a nation into a police
state, as Viktor Orban has done in Hungary

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.069
or Putin and Russia in order to maintain their
power.

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:39.610
Those are the choices we're facing right now
when it comes to sometimes what you might

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.049
call the conventional wisdom around oligarchy,
which is often not.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:45.970
It might be conventional, but it's not necessarily
accurate.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:52.639
There's this idea that the biggest acceleration
in this oligarchical overclass, so to speak,

00:03:52.639 --> 00:03:59.150
was during industrialization, in part because
of the ability to really concentrate wealth

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:04.519
among those who could own a lot of those factories
and take advantage of the industrialization

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:05.519
that was taking place.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:09.499
Is it accurate to point that is the biggest
moment of acceleration of this or would that

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:11.319
not be right?

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:17.390
Well, technological changes always have massive
societal impacts.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:22.140
I mean, you know, you can you can find that
all the way back to the agricultural revolution,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:28.860
the Bronze Age, the Iron Age, the Bronze Age,
the, you know, the invention of steel, et

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.860
cetera.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.380
And and typically those kinds of changes as
the society gets reordered.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:41.960
You have a class within society that succeeds
in grabbing as much of the pie as they can.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.510
The technological innovation in the in the
eighteen hundreds in the early eighteen hundreds

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.690
was the invention by Eli Whitney of the Cotton
Gin.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.580
You know, the hardest part of the bottleneck
in the production of cotton is getting those

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.510
seeds out of a bowl of cotton.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:05:01.500
They're really hard to pull out and the cotton
gin could do what fifty enslaved people could

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.961
do in the same amount of time.

00:05:02.961 --> 00:05:07.330
So the big plantations, which could afford
the cotton gins, were able there fifty times

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.670
more efficient and that's how they wiped out
their small competitors.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:15.740
Similarly, in the in the late eighteen hundreds,
early nineteen hundreds, you saw people like

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:21.300
Carnegie and Rockefeller and and the Dupont
family and Mr. Dupont.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.510
I forget his first name.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:23.510
Alfred definitely.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:24.510
No, it was Alfred.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:30.140
In any case, you saw these guys doing essentially
the same thing, monopolizing the wealth and

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.470
then converting that economic power into political
power.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.480
And that's that's what we've seen the billionaire
class.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.400
Now, in the last 40 years in the United States,
how do you apply this analysis to what we've

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.840
seen over the last year during this pandemic
where we've seen when it comes to business,

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:52.790
we've seen very large businesses that already
had certain degrees of infrastructure in place

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:57.540
sweep up even more market share as a lot of
small businesses, mom and Pops and others,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:02.050
for a variety of different reasons, have gone
under likely permanently in the cases of many

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.500
of them.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:12.970
Well, it's it we're witnessing a another reconsolidation
this time in part because of the pandemic,

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:18.800
but also in large part because, you know,
in 1983, Ronald Reagan stopped enforcing the

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.342
antitrust laws in this country.

00:06:20.342 --> 00:06:23.800
And we haven't had a president since then
that did enforce them.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:29.240
So, you know, if the if the NFL said, you
know, we're just not going to enforce laws

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.610
against people punching each other in the
gut as they run by or ripping facemasks off,

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.610
pretty soon, eventually you're going to have
the team that's the best at punching people

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:44.510
and ripping facemasks off is going to be the
team that's winning all the games in the NFL.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.510
That's what's happened here.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:49.220
I mean, you know, it might take a while before
you get there because everybody considers

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.150
it kind of distasteful.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:56.670
But when Reagan changed the rules of business,
he really set us up on this trajectory to

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.680
where we're at right now.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.680
You mentioned the two ways that oligarchy
can go.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:08.570
It can be defeated or it can become the police
states that you described or born Putin, et

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:09.570
cetera.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.910
What tends to determine whether it goes one
way or the other, the people, whether the

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:19.230
you know, typically what what precipitates
the crisis that flips oligarchy in one direction

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:24.210
or the other is is is usually an economic
