00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:47.089
from that in which the successes during FDR
and the New Deal era took place.

00:09:47.089 --> 00:09:51.730
And so it really needs to be a different road
map for achieving maybe a similar outcome.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:55.240
Do you agree with that, that circumstances
have changed to such a degree that the playbook

00:09:55.240 --> 00:10:01.139
must be different to some degree or not at
all or completely?

00:10:01.139 --> 00:10:09.220
What FDR did was he repudiated the economic
policies of Warren Harding and Calvin Coolidge

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:11.639
and Herbert Hoover.

00:10:11.639 --> 00:10:16.670
Those policies were almost identical to the
ones that Ronald Reagan put into place that

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.759
we're confronting right now.

00:10:18.759 --> 00:10:24.110
When Herbert Hoover ran for president, his
two big sales pitches were, number one, a

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:25.110
return to normalcy.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.110
That was his slogan.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:30.259
What he meant by that was dropping that top
tax rate of ninety one percent that Woodrow

00:10:30.259 --> 00:10:35.690
Wilson had put in during World War One, back
down to twenty five percent, which he did.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:40.139
And his second one was more business and government,
less government and business, in other words,

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:44.760
privatize and deregulate, which was Reagan's
agenda, which he did.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.519
And that took us to the Great Depression,
the generation before that.

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:56.930
In the 90s, the supply siders so-called had
this theory called the Horsens Sparrow theory,

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:02.850
which was that because back then people didn't
have cars, they rode horses and.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:10.249
The streets in in every town in America were
filled with horse piles of horse manure and

00:11:10.249 --> 00:11:14.999
and sparrows love to pick you the occasional
bit of undigested grain out of them.

00:11:14.999 --> 00:11:20.199
And so the theory was, if you feed the horses,
more oats, more undigested oats will come

00:11:20.199 --> 00:11:23.649
out the back end, which will feed the sparrows.

00:11:23.649 --> 00:11:28.990
In other words, give the rich people their
tax cuts and that will trickle down to the

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:29.990
average worker.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:35.259
Yeah, sort of a farmhouse, farmhouse trickle
down, recycled about three times in the last,

00:11:35.259 --> 00:11:36.259
what?

00:11:36.259 --> 00:11:37.259
One hundred and eighty years.

00:11:37.259 --> 00:11:39.360
One hundred and sixty years.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.890
What should the playbook be for the people
at this point in time?

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:51.309
Well, what FDR did is he went back to classic
economics, Adam Smith economics and and in

00:11:51.309 --> 00:12:00.029
that he said that basically what drives an
economy is demand, not supply.

00:12:00.029 --> 00:12:04.009
And demand is aggregate demand is the phrase
economists use.

00:12:04.009 --> 00:12:07.509
It's it's a fancy word that really just means
wages.

00:12:07.509 --> 00:12:13.759
So if we want to get our economy back in order,
what we need to do is get money into the hands

00:12:13.759 --> 00:12:15.819
of average people.

00:12:15.819 --> 00:12:22.660
And to a certain extent, that means, you know,
going back to to top tax brackets that that

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:30.279
are substantial, you know, well over 50 percent
and making sure that when people do work,

00:12:30.279 --> 00:12:35.910
they're properly and appropriate, appropriately
renumerated paid for that.

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.500
So you've got the 15 dollar minimum wage.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:42.959
We need to bring back labor unions, which
pretty much nobody is talking about right

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:46.100
now, but it really should be at the top of
the agenda.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:51.491
And strengthen workplace protections and worker
protections right across the board, and we

00:12:51.491 --> 00:12:53.259
need to strengthen the social safety net.

00:12:53.259 --> 00:12:56.240
We're the only developed country in the world
that doesn't consider health care.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:57.240
Right.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:58.880
And offer basically free health care to everybody.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.980
We're the only developed we're the only developed
country in the world that has medical debt

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.129
as a cause of personal bankruptcy.

00:13:05.129 --> 00:13:08.399
We're the only developed country in the world
that has student debt.

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:12.870
Anything close to what we have every other
developed country in the world offers either

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:17.160
free or very inexpensive college to to anybody
who can qualify.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.269
We don't.

00:13:18.269 --> 00:13:19.550
It's insane.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:24.250
We need to we need to go back to the foundation
foundational economics.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:26.939
This was stuff that literally Thomas Jefferson
was pitching.

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:30.320
He started the first free university in the
United States, the University of Virginia,

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.639
and was very proud of the fact that it was
completely free.

00:13:33.639 --> 00:13:35.379
He thought that would build America.

00:13:35.379 --> 00:13:37.610
He was right.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:41.420
It seems that one of the big propaganda victories
that the economic right I mean, going back

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:47.829
to the time maybe Reagan forward when it comes
to these supply side trickle down idea, is

00:13:47.829 --> 00:13:52.671
one of the biggest propaganda victories they've
had is pushing out the understanding of the

00:13:52.671 --> 00:13:54.649
demand side of economics completely.

00:13:54.649 --> 00:13:58.699
And you see it even when minimum wage is discussed,
you hear the discussion of, well, that's going

00:13:58.699 --> 00:14:02.689
to increase costs for the businesses and they'll
have to lay people off and they won't be able

00:14:02.689 --> 00:14:04.279
to afford employees.

00:14:04.279 --> 00:14:13.269
It ignores what which to some degree ignores
that labor is only one at one input cost for

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:14.699
the product or service that's being produced.

00:14:14.699 --> 00:14:19.220
But it also ignores the fact that you're creating
demand side stimulus and all of a sudden all

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:23.110
of those employees have more money that they
can spend at other businesses.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:29.290
It's it has been a huge victory to completely
make it a one sided conversation, has it not?

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:30.350
That's right.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.910
And and you're absolutely right.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.730
And it's not an accidental prop..

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.050
Propaganda victory.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.110
Lewis Powell laid this out in nineteen seventy
one.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:44.579
I documented in this book on on American oligarchy
and and then Nixon the next year put him on

00:14:44.579 --> 00:14:51.550
the Supreme Court, where he proceeded in a
series of decisions to cement these policies

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:52.550
in place.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:15:00.209
But and groups like the Heritage Foundation
